  :receipt/body
  "cost-coscientist iteration-02 ranked h7 — stop parsing every value on every
   request — as the top UNBLOCKED item, worth 45% of a served read. This takes
   the safe half of it.

   ## What was there

   `decode2` recovers a stored value with `edn/read-string` TWICE. The entity
   model stores `stored-o` as `(pr-str original-value)`, and hot-datoms hands
   back `v_edn` = `(pr-str stored-o)`. So the INNER layer is real EDN — keywords,
   sets, nested maps — and has to stay with the EDN reader.

   **The OUTER layer is always a string literal.** It is never a map, never a
   keyword, never anything but `\"...\"`. The EDN reader was being used to
   unquote a quoted string, character by character, for 4.9 MB of seed on every
   request.

   ## What replaced it

   `JSON.parse`. An EDN string literal and a JSON string are the same grammar
   over the escapes `pr-str` emits, and JSON.parse is native: **140 ms -> 2 ms
   on a 1.9 MB literal, ~70x.**

   The two grammars differ over exactly one thing this domain can produce: raw
   control characters, which `pr-str` emits UNESCAPED and JSON forbids. There,
   JSON.parse *throws* — it does not return something else. So the fallback to
   `edn/read-string` is the entire difference between the two readers here.

   Property-tested before relying on it: **20,000 random strings** (raw control
   characters, lone surrogates, astral planes). JSON.parse never silently
   disagreed — it returned the identical string or threw — and `edn/read-string`
   recovered **100%** of the throws. A `string?` guard sends anything that is
   not a string literal to the authoritative reader, because JSON.parse returns
   a NUMBER for `123` and nil for `null` where the EDN reader returns 123 and the
   symbol `null`: that one WOULD be a silent divergence.

   ## Measured on production's own blocks

   84 blocks, 11.46 MB, at the real head. Baseline and patched builds run
   ALTERNATELY, because this workstation runs many concurrent agent sessions and
   a one-shot number is not comparable across runs. **Phase A is the control** —
   untouched code — so B/A normalises for machine load:

       round   baseline B/A   patched B/A
       1            3.166        2.246     (first run of the session; JIT warm-up)
       2            2.869        2.108
       3            2.875        2.030
       4            2.862        1.931

   **-28% of phase B.** `mirror-check phase-b-equals-shipped-hydrate?` was true
   in every round, so the fold still produces the identical map.

   ## Measured in production, after deploying

   Same probe as iteration-02: `wrangler tail` against
   `atproto.kotobase.net/xrpc/com.atproto.repo.describeRepo`, a public-read
   surface that performs the full shared-graph hydrate.

       before  n=10  median 2397  mean 2462  min 2114  max 3689
       after   n=10  median 2058  mean 2026  min 1422  max 2583

   **Median -14.1%, mean -17.7%.** Stated honestly: n=10 each and the ranges
   overlap (the patched max, 2583, is above the baseline min, 2114). The shift
   of the whole distribution is the claim, not any single pair.

   Cost per million served reads: **$78.48 -> $71.70 (-8.6%)**. The Neo4j Aura
   crossover moves 828K -> 907K queries/month.

   ## Why this was worth doing before the big one

   It changes no contract. Deferring value parsing (the rest of h7) and
   materialising the seed (h1) both change what the synchronous LocalStore is
   handed; this changes only how a string literal is turned into a string. It
   stacks under both, and the remaining headroom is still large — the bench's
   `decode-cbor-opaque-floor`, which parses no values at all, is **5.75 cpu-ms**
   against phase B's 780-900.

   ## Found by running it

   `scripts/fetch-graph-blocks.cljs` — the bench's own documented step 1 — had
   an **unclosed ns docstring** since 53b8884 (2026-08-17). The reader swallowed
   the whole header and died on a bare symbol, so the script had not run since
   that commit. Nothing reported this, because nothing ran it: the phase bench
   quotes it in a `## Reproduce` block that no gate executes. Fixed here.

   Its first symptom was a lie: `Invalid symbol: /tmp/kb-blocks`, pointing at the
   argument I passed, on a line whose string literal was fine. I spent three
   attempts working around nbb argument handling before testing the script with
   NO arguments at all and seeing it fail identically.

   ## Verification

   130 tests / 821 assertions / 0 failures.

   The three new tests discriminate: removing the fallback and the `string?`
   guard gives **6 failures and 0 compile errors**, naming `fallback must
   recover control char 31` and the `null` / `[1 2]` cases — exactly what was
   removed. Restoring returns to 0.

   ## Still open

   `kotoba-lang/kotobase-worker-shell` carries its own copy of this file with the
   double parse intact. It is a separate repo and deployment; this receipt does
   not claim it was fixed."
